**The Arity of Thought: Predicting Relational Errors and Their Rescue**

**Verdict: conditional go for a computational-cognition paper; no-go for claiming attention theory explains “four.”** Arity means how many arguments a relation combines in one processing step. It matters because six visible objects can require several easy comparisons or one difficult integration. The strongest opportunity is predicting **specific errors and selective recovery**, beyond accounts based on feature confusion, learned representations, and sequential computation.

**What the audit permits.** [AUDIT §§2, 3, 5](/Users/neo/repos/polyformer/research/meta/survive/AUDIT.md) establishes equal class-conditional **means** for additive lower-order scores on its specified distribution—not identical distributions or classification impossibility. The coupling result constrains fixed-representation attention logits: scores before probability normalization. Contextual representations, nonlinear readouts, and additional layers can escape. Cubic terms and Newton power sums supply particular solutions; neither implies a biological four-argument ceiling.

**Prior art that materially limits novelty**

- [**Halford, Wilson & Phillips (1998, Behavioral and Brain Sciences)**](https://staff.aist.go.jp/steven.phillips/papers/relational_complexity.pdf) defined complexity through the hardest step of the actual strategy, allowing chunking and sequential decomposition; they already proposed tensor and timing-based mechanisms. [**Halford, Baker, McCredden & Bain (2005, Psychological Science 16:70–76)**](https://www.researchgate.net/publication/8070322_How_Many_Variables_Can_Humans_Process) found declining graph-interaction performance; the five-way result was 12/22 correct on one problem per participant, not a universal bound.
- [**Andrews, Birney & Halford (2006, Memory & Cognition 34:1325–1340)**](https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.3758/BF03193275.pdf) found relational-processing measures predicted relative-clause comprehension beyond simpler comprehension, whereas memory-span measures did not survive that control.
- [**Smolensky (1990, Artificial Intelligence 46:159–216)**](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2017/02/Smolensky-90-AI-Tensor-Product-Variable-Binding-and-the-Representation-of-Symbolic-Structures-in-Connectionist-Systems.pdf) introduced tensor-product binding: multiplying role and content vectors to preserve who occupies which role. [**Hummel & Holyoak (2003, Psychological Review 110:220–264)**](https://reasoninglab.psych.ucla.edu/wp-content/uploads/sites/273/2021/04/Hummel-Holyoak.2003.pdf) used synchronized activity in LISA to bind roles, support analogy, and impose capacity constraints. [**Doumas, Hummel & Sandhofer (2008, Psychological Review 115:1–43)**](https://labs.psychology.illinois.edu/~jehummel/pubs/DORA_PR_2008.pdf) introduced DORA, learning relational representations through comparison. Neural implementation and representation-dependent rescue are established territory.
- [**Webb, Frankland et al., including Cohen (2024, Trends in Cognitive Sciences 28:829–843; arXiv:2309.06629)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2309.06629v3) synthesized the relational bottleneck: restricting a pathway to relationships rather than object details improves abstraction. This restricts information content, not arity. [**Webb, Sinha & Cohen (2021, ICLR; arXiv:2012.14601)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2012.14601) demonstrated novel-object generalization through recurrent memory binding. [**Kerg et al. (2022, arXiv:2206.05056)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2206.05056) obtained strong transfer with CoRelNet’s simple similarity representations.
- [**Altabaa, Webb, Cohen & Lafferty (2024, ICLR; arXiv:2304.00195)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2304.00195) introduced Abstractors, which extract relations into separate symbolic representations, including successful SET classification. Pairwise relation extraction followed by nonlinear processing already handles some apparently higher-order tasks.
- [**Webb, Holyoak & Lu (2023, Nature Human Behaviour 7:1526–1541; arXiv:2212.09196)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2212.09196v2) compared GPT-3 and humans on matrices, letter strings, verbal analogies, and stories. Their public repository contains human results. Crucially, several independent matrix rules do not automatically constitute a relation of that arity.
- [**Fesser, Ektefaie, Fang, Kakade & Zitnik (2026, ICML; arXiv:2604.12176)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2604.12176) introduced REL and found increasing-arity failures despite fixed entity count and additional inference compute. This largely preempts “LLMs have relational-complexity limits.” [**Shang, Kreiman & Sompolinsky (2026, Scientific Reports 16:22146; arXiv:2502.17382)**](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13369344/) related representation geometry to transfer and human difficulty in simplified matrices. Human–model difficulty correlation alone is also insufficient novelty.
- [**Campbell et al. (2024, NeurIPS; arXiv:2411.00238)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2411.00238) linked visual-language reasoning failures to feature interference and demonstrated rescue by reducing it. [**Nurisso et al. (2025, arXiv:2506.14797)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2506.14797) derived generalization–identification tradeoffs from limited representational resolution. These are mandatory alternatives to an arity account.
- [**Sanford, Hsu & Telgarsky (2023, NeurIPS; arXiv:2306.02896)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2306.02896) proved restricted shallow-transformer limitations and higher-order separations. [**Kozachinskiy et al. (2025, NeurIPS; arXiv:2501.19215)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2501.19215) strengthened one-layer limitations and introduced Strassen attention. [**Chakrabarti, Pitassi & Alman (2026, arXiv:2602.02422)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2602.02422) generalized higher-order attention and its expressivity/computation tradeoffs. A generic attention-arity hierarchy is occupied territory.
- [**Gong & Zhang (2024, arXiv:2409.10715)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2409.10715) associated toy-transformer working-memory failures with dispersed attention; additional layers/heads substantially relieved them. [**Yang et al. (2025, arXiv:2502.20332)**](https://arxiv.org/html/2502.20332) identified successive abstraction, induction, and retrieval mechanisms inside ordinary LLMs. Both caution against equating pairwise primitives with pairwise effective computation.
- [**Hearne, Robinson, Cocchi & Ito (2025, bioRxiv:10.1101/2025.10.29.685457)**](https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.10.29.685457) report transformer–human neural alignment during Latin-square reasoning. Abstract verified; full text and reusable data **unverified**. This remains an important novelty check.

**Strongest version: an exact prediction, followed by a causal intervention.**

For four available candidate pairs, assume a single choice stage with fixed encodings:

\[
L(q,j,k)=a(q,j)+b(q,k)+c(j,k),\qquad P(j,k\mid q)\propto e^{L(q,j,k)}.
\]

The audit implies

\[
C(q)=\log\frac{P(1,1\mid q)P(2,2\mid q)}
{P(1,2\mid q)P(2,1\mid q)}
\]

cannot change with rule cue \(q\): all cue-dependent pairwise terms cancel. A genuine three-way term can change it.

This ratio measures whether the cue changes how two candidate components combine. It supplies an exact error-pattern restriction, stronger than predicting lower accuracy. Extend through alternating contrasts over more roles. The cancellation itself is standard interaction algebra; the prospective contribution is successfully predicting behavior and interventions.

**Assumptions we would add:** fixed encodings, this probability rule, and limited processing time. Strategy mixtures or contextualization can violate the restriction without primitive higher-order attention. Behavioral success identifies an effective interaction, not cubic neurons. Even determining whether six binary inputs contain an odd number of ones permits repeated two-input updates. Nothing selects four without independently constrained resource assumptions.

**Decisive test.** Generate tasks with 3–6 interacting roles, six displayed entities, constant answer count, independently varied feature similarity, and matched lower-order statistics. Respect the audit’s sampling assumptions; distinct-card SET tables do not inherit its blindness theorem. Include cue-controlled pair selection and a second task family. Count the cue explicitly; rule counts cannot substitute for arity.

Compare single-stage pairwise, recurrent/deeper pairwise, Abstractor, genuine joint-term, and higher-order-value models under matched training, parameter, and compute budgets. A value model computes joint features before selection; it tests whether improvement requires higher-order routing specifically. Include interference and explicit sequential-rule accounts. Widening the same additive score cannot remove the probability restriction; changing representations through recurrence can.

Register the human prediction before collecting data: under deadlines, errors exhibit the missing interaction; extra time or a supplied intermediate relation selectively restores it. Similarity reduction should chiefly repair interference errors. Calibrate response noise and processing time separately, then predict held-out choices and response times. Require better prediction of actual choices than competing models, not another matching accuracy curve. Compare intermediate-relation support with a matched visual/memory aid that preserves integration demand.

**Existing data and cost.** Begin with [Webb’s public human results](https://github.com/taylorwwebb/emergent_analogies_LLM) and REL for model evaluation. Recode actual strategies, rule length, and similarity before asking whether arity adds explanatory power. Published Halford/Andrews results offer external checks, but raw trials are **unverified**. [SimplifiedRPM’s code/data archive](https://github.com/ShaneShang/SimplifiedRPM-Visual-Reasoning) exists; human trial availability is **unverified**. None verified supplies the complete choice/deadline design above. Existing data can reject the proposal cheaply; they cannot establish the proposed mechanism by themselves.

Estimate one week and **$200–500** for the model/data rejection test. If predictions separate, simulate statistical power, then approximately **120–160 adults × 20 minutes** costs **$800–1,200**, including platform overhead. Allow **4–6 weeks and $1,500–3,000 overall**, excluding salary and ethics delays.

**Kill:** stop before recruiting if recurrent pairwise/Abstractor or interference/rule-length accounts make indistinguishable predictions. Reject the human claim if held-out choices show no incremental predictive improvement or selective intervention effect.

Internal recurrence preserves no-chain-of-thought; written intermediate reasoning relaxes condition 4 and buys explicit decomposition. The cognitive route defers interpreter/certification and language-preservation goals and likely exceeds condition 12’s week. A model-only causal study is cheaper, but cannot establish the human capacity mechanism.1. **What does language comprehension preserve: words, or distinctions needed later?**

   **Old question and data.** Why are nested sentences difficult when their words are predictable? Use StructuralCost’s 475 readers and working-memory scores, with Natural Stories for independent replication. *Nerode equivalence* groups histories producing identical outputs under every allowed continuation. It supplies precise controls for what memory compression loses within a specified grammar.

   **Decisive analysis.** Fine-tune matched small language models under equal memory budgets: retain recent words, selectively retain words, or retain unfinished grammatical obligations. Derive the required distinctions from finite grammars. Freeze predictions before testing held-out structural conditions and participants. Require correct interactions between sentence structure and memory capacity, plus transfer to natural text, beyond differences in word predictability. Remove and restore the proposed memory content inside models.

   **Prior art.** [Nusbaumer et al. (2026, Findings of EMNLP)](https://aclanthology.org/2026.findings-emnlp.1360.pdf) released StructuralCost and documented models’ underestimated integration costs. [Futrell et al. (2021, *Language Resources and Evaluation*)](https://sites.socsci.uci.edu/~rfutrell/papers/futrell2021natural.pdf) released Natural Stories’ reading times for unusual constructions. [Hahn, Futrell, Levy & Gibson (2022, *PNAS*)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9618130/) already explained recursive processing through optimized word retention. [Kajikawa, Ai, Kuribayashi & Wilcox (2026, arXiv:2609.32119)](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.32119v1) connected discourse tracking to reading-time prediction.

   **Review case.** Nature Human Behaviour/PNAS potential comes from identifying what memory preserves and predicting specific failures across archives. Assumption: the grammatical abstraction captures relevant human distinctions. Kill it if gains are only aggregate fit. StructuralCost lacks item-level comprehension accuracy; use reading times. Its paper reports public data, but download access remained **unverified** here.

2. **Which memory mechanisms do human choices actually require?**

   **Old question and data.** Can behavior distinguish gradual reward averaging from memory for particular events? Reuse Eckstein’s public reward-learning records—862 participants, 617,871 trials—and independent human datasets from Ji-An’s study.

   **Decisive analysis.** Fit neural and compact symbolic competitors, including LLM-proposed mechanisms. In bounded discrete tasks, certify which models give identical future choice probabilities and find continuations separating the others. Search for those diagnostic histories in held-out archived participants and tasks. Compare running reward estimates, latest-event memory and memory for global reward context. Distinguish mechanisms required by observations from interchangeable descriptions; quantify uncertainty and missing coverage.

   **Prior art.** [Eckstein, Summerfield, Daw & Miller (2026, *Nature Human Behaviour*)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13212159/) showed advantages from flexible memory variables beyond standard reward estimates. [Ji-An, Benna & Mattar (2025, *Nature*)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12390849/) extracted interpretable strategies from tiny recurrent networks. [Kasenberg et al. (2026, bioRxiv:2026.05.18.725921; preprint)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13228651/) already used LLM-generated programs to discover mechanisms and validate predictions through reanalysis. [Shalizi & Crutchfield (2001, *Journal of Statistical Physics*; arXiv:cond-mat/9907176)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9907176) established minimal predictive-state theory.

   **Review case.** The contribution must be a substantive correction to what human learning data identify, replicated across tasks. Adding certification to model discovery is insufficient. Assumption: archived trials contain enough separating cases; test this before training. Exact claims concern specified models, never the complete human mind.

3. **Do languages compensate across grammatical cues because comprehension has limited memory?**

   **Old question and data.** Linguistic typology compares grammatical systems across languages. Use Grambank and [Universal Dependencies corpora](https://universaldependencies.org/) to ask whether case marking, word-order flexibility and structural arrangement jointly determine recoverability of “who did what to whom.”

   **Decisive analysis.** Construct finite grammar families communicating identical messages. Count future-relevant distinctions needed to recover participant roles; compare these predictions with communication-efficiency models and models containing only pairwise feature relationships. Fine-tune matched LLMs on alternative grammars, with randomly initialized models controlling prior linguistic exposure. The decisive result is a predicted three-way compensation pattern, including exceptions, in language families excluded from fitting. Control ancestry, geography, annotation and missingness.

   **Prior art.** [Hahn, Jurafsky & Futrell (2020, *PNAS*)](https://sites.socsci.uci.edu/~rfutrell/papers/hahn2020universals.pdf) recovered word-order regularities through efficient-communication optimization. [Verkerk et al. (2025 online/2026 issue, *Nature Human Behaviour*)](https://sfb1102.uni-saarland.de/sfbunisb/uploads/2025/11/s41562-025-02325-z.pdf) tested 191 proposed universals across 2,430 Grambank languages while controlling ancestry and geography. [Li & Liu (2025, *Entropy* 27:1128)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12651683/) already examined morphology, order and directionality jointly.

   **Review case.** Nature Human Behaviour potential requires a new quantitative constraint explaining exceptions and unseen combinations. A correlation map or rediscovered case/order tradeoff will not suffice. Assumption: finite-fragment memory costs transfer to real grammars; rare combinations cannot simply be labelled impossible. This has broader reach than #1 but greater linguistic and statistical risk.

4. **When does learning a new representation make a difficult concept easy?**

   **Old question and data.** Category learning studies how people group examples. Reuse public [Psych-101 category trials](https://huggingface.co/datasets/marcelbinz/Psych-101), including Badham’s younger/older-adult experiments, plus published concept-difficulty aggregates. *Interaction order* means how many features jointly enter a relation in a specified representation. Changing that representation can change the order, making it a candidate explanation for learning transitions.

   **Decisive analysis.** Separate shortest-rule complexity, interaction order and similarity-based grouping on the actual archived stimuli. Compare matched ordinary-attention, higher-order-attention and learned-representation models. Certify finite stimulus calculations. Predict trial-by-trial confusions and learning transitions in held-out category families, especially where candidate explanations disagree. Test whether learned intermediate representations explain those changes.

   **Prior art.** [Feldman (2000, *Nature*)](https://courses.media.mit.edu/2003spring/mas963/feldman_nature.pdf) related concept difficulty to shortest Boolean descriptions. [Feldman (2006, *Journal of Mathematical Psychology* 50:339–368)](https://www.researchwithrutgers.org/en/publications/an-algebra-of-human-concept-learning/) already decomposed concepts by feature degree—an important omission from the first-pass memo; abstract verified, full text **unverified**. [Badham, Sanborn & Maylor (2017, *Psychology and Aging*)](https://wrap.warwick.ac.uk/88425/7/WRAP-deficits-category-older-rule-based-clustering-Maylor-2017.pdf) found larger age deficits for less complex categories, supporting a clustering explanation. [Binz et al. (2025, *Nature*)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12390832/) introduced Psych-101 and fine-tuned Centaur to predict behavior.

   **Review case.** A representation-change mechanism explaining difficult counterexamples could support Nature Human Behaviour/PNAS. “Interaction order predicts difficulty” is insufficiently novel. Assumption: archives contain enough contrasting learning trajectories. Split studies independently of Centaur’s training exposure. The audit’s equal-class-means result supplies no general impossibility theorem for ordinary attention.

5. **Does brain–language-model agreement depend on the computations comprehension needs?**

   **Old question and data.** Computational neuroscience asks whether similar responses imply shared computation. Reuse processed Narratives brain scans and Pereira’s sentence responses; neither requires recruitment.

   **Decisive analysis.** Build models with preserved or selectively erased memory for pending grammatical distinctions, matched on average language performance. Test whether those components specifically explain brain responses beyond word timing, position and simple semantic features. Hold out whole stimuli and participants where the design permits; replicate across datasets. Compare equally disruptive interventions to irrelevant components. Certification applies to finite model tasks; the brain comparison remains observational.

   **Prior art.** [Nastase et al. (2021, *Scientific Data* 8:250)](https://www.honeylab.org/pdfs/nastase_etal_narratives_2021.pdf) released Narratives: 345 participants listening to 27 stories. [Pereira et al. (2018, *Nature Communications* 9:963)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5840373/) demonstrated decoding of linguistic meaning from brain activity. [Hadidi et al. (2026, *Nature Communications* 17:5769)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13324000/) showed that methodological choices and overlooked stimulus variables can inflate apparent alignment. [Jia (2026, arXiv:2605.14025; preprint)](https://arxiv.org/html/2605.14025v1) already proposed relational and mechanism-removal controls.

   **Review case.** Potential rests on a positive, selective computational explanation replicated across archives. Another alignment score or confound critique is crowded territory. Assumption: existing stimuli contain enough diagnostic contrasts. Model interventions establish causality inside models, not brains. This is the highest execution-risk option for a solo researcher.

**Start with #1; retain #2 as the theory-first alternative.** The ranking weighs scientific payoff against solo feasibility. Across all five, I assume publication value may precede the internal program generator, universal executor and investor demo—conditions 3, 7 and 11. The one-week/$1–2k condition supports a decisive pilot, not a completed flagship paper. Nature-level ambition would require a general principle demonstrated across independent settings; a small predictive improvement would not establish it.
